# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/reported-as-premise: C2 (S6): C2 rests on P6, which reports what aristotle says: a work's account of another's claim is not its premise; if the work holds it, it says so on a line of its own
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 has "aversion_egoistic"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "it is rather the end of Egoistic than of Universalistic Hedonism to which Common Sense feels an aversion" is false. Is S4, "It is, in fact, rather the end of Egoistic than of Universalistic Hedonism, to which Common Sense feels an aversion.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2 has "indiv_narrow" and "ultimate_marks → (indiv_narrow → ¬indiv_has_marks)"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 has "indiv_narrow" and "ultimate_marks" and "ultimate_marks ∧ indiv_narrow → ¬indiv_has_marks"; they differ when only "one's individual happiness is so narrow and limited, of such necessarily brief duration, and so shifting and insecure while it lasts" is true, which the first allows; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 has "ultimate_marks" and "indiv_narrow"
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "one's individual happiness is so narrow and limited, of such necessarily brief duration, and so shifting and insecure while it lasts" is true and "the characteristics we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good are breadth, lasting duration and security" and "one's individual happiness possesses the characteristics we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good" are false. Is S6, "It does not possess the characteristics which, as Aristotle says, we “divine” to belong to Ultimate Good: being (so far, at least, as it can be empirically foreseen) so narrow and limited, of such necessarily brief duration, and so shifting and insecure while it lasts.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2 has "univ_vast_secure"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 have "univ_vast_secure" and "univ_vast_secure → univ_satisfies"; they differ when only "Universal Happiness is vast and comparatively secure" is true, which the first allows
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "Universal Happiness is vast and comparatively secure" is true and "Universal Happiness seems an End that satisfies our imagination and sustains our resolution" is false. Is S7, "But Universal Happiness, desirable consciousness or feeling for the innumerable multitude of sentient beings, present and to come, seems an End that satisfies our imagination by its vastness, and sustains our resolution by its comparative security.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2 has "¬indiv_has_marks → indiv_unsatisfactory" and "univ_vast_secure → univ_satisfies"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 has "offends_regard → aversion_egoistic" and "¬indiv_has_marks → indiv_unsatisfactory"; they differ when only "this offends both our sympathetic and our rational regard for others' happiness", "one's individual happiness possesses the characteristics we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good", "one's individual happiness is an unsatisfactory mark for one's supreme aim", "Universal Happiness is vast and comparatively secure" and "Universal Happiness seems an End that satisfies our imagination and sustains our resolution" are true, which the first allows; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 has "ultimate_marks ∧ indiv_narrow → ¬indiv_has_marks" and "¬indiv_has_marks → indiv_unsatisfactory"
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2 has "univ_satisfies ∧ indiv_unsatisfactory"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 have "univ_satisfies"; they differ when only "Universal Happiness seems an End that satisfies our imagination and sustains our resolution" is true, which the second allows
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered yes, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "Universal Happiness seems an End that satisfies our imagination and sustains our resolution" is true and "one's individual happiness is an unsatisfactory mark for one's supreme aim" is false. Is the conclusion, "But Universal Happiness, desirable consciousness or feeling for the innumerable multitude of sentient beings, present and to come, seems an End that satisfies our imagination by its vastness, and sustains our resolution by its comparative security.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if this offends both our sympathetic and our rational regard for others' happiness, then it is rather the end of Egoistic than of Universalistic Hedonism to which Common Sense feels an aversion"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2 does not
sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if this offends both our sympathetic and our rational regard for others' happiness, then it is rather the end of Egoistic than of Universalistic Hedonism to which Common Sense feels an aversion"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/negation: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.2 P7 (S6): the sentence says "insecure", but P7 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the characteristics we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good are breadth, lasting duration and security"
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/negation: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r2.3 P7 (S6): the sentence says "insecure", but P7 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the characteristics we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good are breadth, lasting duration and security"
